            <ArticleTitle>Idiopathic stabbing headache associated with monocular visual loss.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>745-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Idiopathic stabbing headache, which is a brief, sharp, severe jabbing pain that is confined to the head, responds well to treatment with indomethacin sodium. It may occur as a primary entity but more likely is associated with other types of headache, including migraine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Emergency department of a teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 27-year-old man presented to the emergency department with stabbing, sharp pain in the right temporal area associated with complete of loss vision in his right eye. The patient had a history of migraine with aura since 1995.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patient was treated with oxygen inhalation and indomethacin, with complete resolution of his symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A case of idiopathic stabbing headache associated with monocular visual loss was relieved by oxygen and indomethacin therapy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
